Logstash (The Pipeline Processor)
Role: Ingest, transform, and ship data.

Three Pipeline Stages:

text
INPUT → FILTER → OUTPUT

Input Examples:      Filter Examples:        Output Examples:
- Filebeat (beats)   - grok (parse)         - Elasticsearch
- Kafka              - mutate (modify)      - S3
- HTTP               - date (timestamp)     - Slack
- TCP/UDP            - geoip (location)     - Email
Working Configuration Example:

# logstash.conf
input {
  beats {
    port => 5044
    host => "0.0.0.0"
  }
}

filter {
  # Parse nginx logs like: 192.168.1.1 - - [04/May/2026:10:30:15] "GET /api"
  grok {
    match => { "message" => "%{IP:client_ip} - - \[%{HTTPDATE:timestamp}\] \"%{WORD:method} %{URIPATH:uri}\"" }
  }
  
  # Convert response time string to integer
  mutate {
    convert => { "response_time" => "integer" }
  }
  
  # Add geolocation from IP address
  geoip {
    source => "client_ip"
  }
}

output {
  elasticsearch {
    hosts => ["localhost:9200"]
    index => "weblogs-%{+YYYY.MM.dd}"
  }
  
  # Write failed logs to dead letter queue
  dead_letter_queue {
    path => "/var/log/logstash/dlq"
  }
}


The Queue System (Your Zero Data Loss Friend):

Memory Queue (Default): Fast but data loss on crash

Persistent Queue (Production): Writes to disk, survives restarts

Queue Types:

memory: Lossy, low latency

persisted: Durable, medium latency

none: No buffering (dangerous!)